    </div>

    <p><strong>Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) in a single line of code.</strong></p>
    
    <p><code>ydata-profiling</code> delivers an extended analysis of a DataFrame without writing custom code for every chart and statistic. It generates an interactive HTML report containing descriptive statistics, correlation matrices, quantile statistics, and detailed distribution plots.</p>

    <div class="note">
        <strong>Note:</strong> This package was formerly known as <code>pandas-profiling</code>. Please update your environment to <code>ydata-profiling</code> to access the latest features.
    </div>

    <h2>🚀 Key Features</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Type Inference:</strong> Automatically detects numerical, categorical, and boolean features.</li>
        <li><strong>Essentials:</strong> Type, unique values, missing values, most frequent values, variable size.</li>
        <li><strong>Quantile Statistics:</strong> Minimum value, Q1, median, Q3, maximum, range, interquartile range.</li>
        <li><strong>Descriptive Statistics:</strong> Mean, mode, standard deviation, sum, median absolute deviation, coefficient of variation, kurtosis, skewness.</li>
        <li><strong>Histograms:</strong> Visualization of distributions for every variable.</li>
        <li><strong>Correlations:</strong> Spearman, Pearson, Kendall, Cramér's V, and Phik correlation matrices with heatmaps.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing Values:</strong> Matrix, Count, Heatmap, and Dendrogram of missing data patterns.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>📦 Installation</h2>
    <p>You can install <code>ydata-profiling</code> using pip or conda.</p>

    <h3>Using pip</h3>
    <pre><code>pip install ydata-profiling</code></pre>

    <h3>Using conda</h3>
    <pre><code>conda install -c conda-forge ydata-profiling</code></pre>

    <h2>💻 Usage</h2>

    <h3>1. Basic Usage (Generate & Save Report)</h3>
    <p>The most common use case is to load a dataset and save the report as an HTML file.</p>

    <pre><code>import pandas as pd
from ydata_profiling import ProfileReport

# Load your dataset
df = pd.read_csv("my_dataset.csv")

# Generate the profile report
profile = ProfileReport(df, title="Pandas Profiling Report")

# Save to file
profile.to_file("my_report.html")</code></pre>

    <h3>2. Usage in Jupyter Notebooks</h3>
    <p>If you are working inside Jupyter (Lab/Notebook), you can render the widgets directly in the cell.</p>

    <pre><code># Render the interactive widget
profile.to_widgets()

# OR render as an Iframe (better for GitHub sharing)
profile.to_notebook_iframe()</code></pre>

    <h3>3. Usage with Large Datasets (Minimal Mode)</h3>
    <p>Generating correlation matrices and deep statistical analysis can be slow on large datasets (&gt;1 million rows). Use <code>minimal=True</code> to disable expensive computations.</p>

    <pre><code>profile = ProfileReport(df, minimal=True)
profile.to_file("large_dataset_report.html")</code></pre>

    <h2>⚙️ Advanced Configuration</h2>
    <p>You can control exactly what is calculated by passing a configuration dictionary.</p>

    <pre><code>profile = ProfileReport(
    df, 
    title="No Correlations Report", 
    correlations={
        "pearson": {"calculate": False},
        "spearman": {"calculate": False},
        "kendall": {"calculate": False},
        "phi_k": {"calculate": False},
        "cramers": {"calculate": False},
    }
)</code></pre>

    <h2>📊 Command Line Usage</h2>
    <p>You can also use the library directly from the command line to profile a CSV file without writing a Python script.</p>

    <pre><code>ydata-profiling my_dataset.csv report.html</code></pre>

